{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel at 62d3fd2c6bfbed00603bdca1bf9c1ae15bf65bab. The bounded submission queue, with its refusal ordering now proven rather than asserted.\n\nWHAT CHANGED FROM THE REJECTED HEAD. One new test, TestSubmitRefusesAtCapacityBeforeParsing, and nothing else. No production code moved; kernel.go is byte-identical to 183ebe7d.\n\nTHE GAP CODEX FOUND, which was real. The governing request and limits.md both claim the capacity refusal is taken before the signed intent is parsed. The existing TestSubmitRefusesAtCapacityBeforeChaining observes only that the pre-append hook did not run, and that hook sits long after intent.Verify. So a guard moved to just after decoding would leave the whole suite green while making the documented claim false. The ordering was asserted in prose and unproven by test.\n\nHOW THE NEW TEST SETTLES IT. A request whose signed intent cannot be decoded separates the two orderings, because the two refusals are distinguishable. Submitted at capacity it must be refused for capacity, wrapping ErrBackPressure; if the parser ran first it would be refused for being malformed, which does not wrap the sentinel. The test also submits the same malformed request with a slot free and requires a decode refusal there, so it cannot pass on a build where every refusal happens to wrap ErrBackPressure — without that half it would prove nothing about ordering.\n\nVERIFIED BY MUTATION, and the result is worth stating precisely because it reproduces the reviewer's prediction exactly. I inserted intent.Verify before the capacity guard, which is the \"moved after decoding\" placement. TestSubmitRefusesAtCapacityBeforeChaining stayed green. TestSubmitRefusesAtCapacityBeforeParsing failed with \"invalid actor public key length, want ErrBackPressure — the capacity guard runs after the parser\". So the old test is insensitive to the ordering and the new one is not. Mutation reverted, kernel.go verified clean.\n\nWHY I MISSED IT. My four earlier mutations all attacked whether the bound exists — drop it at the app call site, disable the check, change the zero case, make the retry error anonymous. Existence and position are different properties, and a mutation suite built only from removals cannot catch a claim about sequence. Whenever documentation says \"before X\", the probe has to be something that behaves differently on each side of X.\n\nUNCHANGED AND STILL TRUE. MaxQueueDepth bounds submissions inside Submitter.Submit; the refusal at kernel.go:139 precedes parsing, admission hooks, payload-tree writes and chaining; a zero limit is the embedding opt-out; ErrBackPressure deliberately also covers Submit's CAS exhaustion at :538, which codex confirmed is the right call since both mean retryable contention; Rotate keeps its anonymous exhaustion at :398.\n\nGATES at this exact head, uncached: internal/kernel 127.485s, internal/app 26.449s, -race on internal/kernel 165.468s, full go test ./... zero failures, internal/docset 86.219s, go vet clean, go build clean, git diff --check clean, git merge-tree against main 27603774 clean.\n\nRests on the live non-stale predecessor d97eed89, which main carries, and supersedes my own candidate 774efb29 at the rejected head.","body":{"commit":"62d3fd2c6bfbed00603bdca1bf9c1ae15bf65bab","path":"internal/kernel"}}